  "question_text": "LOAD. A bus's LOAD is its betweenness centrality: the number of shortest paths\nbetween all pairs of buses that pass through that bus, counted raw (unnormalized).\nThe \"busiest\" bus is the one with the highest load under this definition.\nLOAD here does NOT mean electrical power demand in MW. Ignore any MW demand\nvalues attached to the grid data.\n\nOVERLOAD CASCADE. Use the Motter-Lai cascading failure model, defined as follows:\n  1. Each bus has a LOAD equal to its betweenness centrality (unnormalized, i.e.\n     raw shortest-path counts) computed on the INITIAL intact network.\n  2. Each bus has a fixed CAPACITY = (1 + alpha) * its INITIAL load, where\n     alpha = 0.2. Capacity is computed once from the intact network and NEVER\n     changes during the cascade.\n  3. Apply the trigger described in the question.\n  4. Recompute every surviving bus's load (betweenness) on the CURRENT surviving\n     network. Any bus whose current load is strictly greater than its capacity\n     is overloaded. Remove all such buses simultaneously. One ROUND is one\n     execution of this step.\n  5. Repeat step 4 until a round produces no new failures.\nWhen the cascade finishes, a bus has FAILED if it is not part of the largest\nconnected component. That covers buses that overloaded and buses that were cut off\nfrom the main network without ever overloading: either way they are out of service.\nThe trigger itself has failed too.\nReport any fraction as a fraction of the number of buses in the largest connected\ncomponent BEFORE the cascade. This grid starts fully connected, so that is all\n118 buses. (This is G = N'/N in Motter-Lai 2002.)\n\nMODIFIED GRID. The question changes the topology before anything fails. The\nMODIFIED grid is the INITIAL network: compute every bus's load and capacity on\nthe modified grid, before applying any trigger. Do not carry over loads or\ncapacities computed on the original topology.\n\nQUESTION: An engineer is considering building one new power line, and has five candidates: 31-81, 48-67, 23-76, 48-82, or 23-81. For each option, consider the upgraded grid and the overload cascade triggered by the failure of bus 68. Which candidate line leaves the largest surviving connected network?",
  "context_blocks": [
    "load",
    "overload",
    "modified_grid"
  ],
